/-!
# `O^{2'}(G)`: the subgroup generated by the elements of odd order

In a finite group, `O^{2'}(G)` is the joint of the normal subgroups of `2`-power index;
equivalently (and this is the definition used here) it is the subgroup generated by the
elements of odd order.  The quotient `G ⧸ O^{2'}(G)` is then a `2`-group, and `O^{2'}(G)` is
characteristic in `G`.

This file sets up the notion and its elementary API, which is used by the reduction of
`Work.ThreePoints.twoTransitive_stabilizer_even`: if `¬ HasFourGroup G` then for every
involution `z` the image of `z` in `G ⧸ O^{2'}(G)` is central.

## Why `¬ HasFourGroup` makes the involutions central mod `O^{2'}`

For any `g : G` and any involution `z`, the element `z * g * z⁻¹ * g⁻¹ = z * (g * z * g⁻¹)` is
a product of the two involutions `z` and `g * z * g⁻¹`.  If they are equal it is `1`; otherwise
`¬ HasFourGroup G` (equivalently: no two distinct involutions commute, i.e. the product of two
distinct involutions has *odd* order) makes it an element of odd order.  Either way it lies in
`O^{2'}(G)`, which says exactly that the image of `z` is central in the quotient.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace Work.ThreePoints

/-- `O^{2'}(G)`: the subgroup generated by the elements of odd order. -/
def twoPrimeResidual (G : Type*) [Group G] : Subgroup G :=
  Subgroup.closure {x : G | Odd (orderOf x)}

/-- Odd-order elements lie in `O^{2'}(G)`. -/
theorem mem_twoPrimeResidual_of_odd_orderOf {G : Type*} [Group G] {x : G}
    (h : Odd (orderOf x)) : x ∈ twoPrimeResidual G :=
  Subgroup.subset_closure h

/- `O^{2'}(G)` is normal in `G`: the generating set `{x | Odd (orderOf x)}` is invariant under
conjugation (because `orderOf` is), and the closure of a conjugation-invariant set is normal.
The formal proof is delegated (`ThreePoints/twoPrimeResidual_normal`). -/

end Work.ThreePoints
